characterized by progressive muscle rigidity, postural instability, and painful anxiety-triggered spasms, believed to be
prevalent in 1 to 2 cases per million1-3. The pathogenesis of the
disease involves various autoantibodies targeting 6 antigens in the

GABAergic neurotransmission pathway, most commonly glutamic
acid decarboxylase (GAD)2-4. Forms of SPS are classified on their
phenotypic presentation or associated disorders, which include
joint subluxations, gait abnormalities, cognitive decline, psychiatric
disorders, respiratory failure, autonomic function, sudden death,

